1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,310
This video is a first of a
series of videos where I want

2
00:00:03,310 --> 00:00:05,900
to talk about embedded
software testing.

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:07,390
This one we're going to go over

4
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:10,165
some introduction to
software testing,

5
00:00:10,165 --> 00:00:12,190
talking about some
concepts, strategies,

6
00:00:12,190 --> 00:00:14,695
and nomenclature associated
with software testing,

7
00:00:14,695 --> 00:00:16,210
and understand the role of

8
00:00:16,210 --> 00:00:20,780
manual and automated testing
for embedded devices.

9
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,700
My experience is that
manual testing is almost

10
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:26,430
always required on
embedded systems

11
00:00:26,430 --> 00:00:27,925
in at least some areas.

12
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:29,860
That's just given the
fact that most of

13
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:32,085
the time we're working
on hardware devices,

14
00:00:32,085 --> 00:00:34,870
and human interaction is

15
00:00:34,870 --> 00:00:38,110
often hard to completely
replace with those devices,

16
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:40,180
and so depending on the size of

17
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:42,970
your team and the capabilities
your automation platform,

18
00:00:42,970 --> 00:00:44,600
there's some level typically

19
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:46,990
of manual testing that
you'll need to do.

20
00:00:46,990 --> 00:00:50,630
In a lot of cases, what
you'll do is start with

21
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:54,605
manual testing for the
hardest portions to automate.

22
00:00:54,605 --> 00:00:56,990
Then perhaps some of those
you might be able to automate

23
00:00:56,990 --> 00:01:00,055
later as you have more time
to run automated testing,

24
00:01:00,055 --> 00:01:03,020
but automated testing
has obvious benefits

25
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:05,825
from the perspective of
repeatability and speed.

26
00:01:05,825 --> 00:01:08,600
If you don't have to
designate someone from

27
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:12,305
your development or test
team to run manual tests,

28
00:01:12,305 --> 00:01:14,680
then obviously you can
run them more often.

29
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:17,375
Automated tests don't care
if you run them at midnight.

30
00:01:17,375 --> 00:01:20,090
For instance, they
can run anytime,

31
00:01:20,090 --> 00:01:22,100
and so there's a lot
of flexibility and

32
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:24,380
the more automated testing
you can get in general,

33
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:27,750
the better that's going
to make your code.

34
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:31,010
There's an international
software testing and

35
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:34,190
qualifications board that has
some documentation I'll go

36
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:35,615
over when I'm talking about

37
00:01:35,615 --> 00:01:40,085
general software test
concepts and principles.

38
00:01:40,085 --> 00:01:43,760
A few things that document
mentions that I think are

39
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:45,560
important to keep in

40
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,525
mind when you're talking
about software testing.

41
00:01:47,525 --> 00:01:49,100
Testing can show the presence of

42
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:50,770
defects but not their absence.

43
00:01:50,770 --> 00:01:52,985
What that means is
that it's not possible

44
00:01:52,985 --> 00:01:55,855
to say that there are no
defects based on testing,

45
00:01:55,855 --> 00:01:57,770
and this is an important concept

46
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:00,650
to understand and to make
sure everybody on your team,

47
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:02,585
especially your
management understands,

48
00:02:02,585 --> 00:02:04,520
that it's not possible
to exhaustively

49
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:06,890
test to the point that
you know there's no bugs.

50
00:02:06,890 --> 00:02:09,245
You need to be strategic
about what you test

51
00:02:09,245 --> 00:02:11,645
to know where you're most
likely to find bugs,

52
00:02:11,645 --> 00:02:13,460
and ensure that you've got

53
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:15,800
adequate coverage
to ensure there

54
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:17,600
aren't bugs in the
areas that are the most

55
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,825
important for your
particular application.

56
00:02:20,825 --> 00:02:22,670
Another testing principal is

57
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:24,635
that the earlier that you test,

58
00:02:24,635 --> 00:02:27,065
the more time and money
in general you can save,

59
00:02:27,065 --> 00:02:29,060
the less times you need

60
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:30,890
to replace products in
the field because you

61
00:02:30,890 --> 00:02:32,555
didn't catch some bug

62
00:02:32,555 --> 00:02:35,060
related to your update
process, for instance,

63
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:39,065
or the less time you have to
spend from your support team

64
00:02:39,065 --> 00:02:41,420
supporting a customer
that hit a bug that

65
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:44,155
you didn't catch in your
development process,

66
00:02:44,155 --> 00:02:46,895
the more money you're going
to save in the long-term,

67
00:02:46,895 --> 00:02:50,545
the earlier you catch
those problems.

68
00:02:50,545 --> 00:02:52,740
Defects are often clustered,

69
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:55,625
if you find one bug they
usually have friends.

70
00:02:55,625 --> 00:02:59,480
The developers are
force of habit,

71
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:02,945
and if they make one
mistake in one place,

72
00:03:02,945 --> 00:03:04,790
it's pretty likely that

73
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:06,230
they or another
developer that they

74
00:03:06,230 --> 00:03:07,970
copied source code from could've

75
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:10,430
made a similar mistake
in another place.

76
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:12,515
Usually when you find one bug,

77
00:03:12,515 --> 00:03:14,330
it's a good idea to
go in and look for

78
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:17,320
similar bugs in other areas.

79
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:19,850
As a developer, it's
definitely something to

80
00:03:19,850 --> 00:03:21,820
think about when
you're fixing a bug,

81
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:25,530
where could I have made
this mistake before?

82
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:28,240
Tests need to be continually

83
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:30,145
developed to continue
to find defects,

84
00:03:30,145 --> 00:03:32,800
and it's what they call
the pesticide paradox,

85
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:35,020
which is an interesting
way to think about it,

86
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:36,430
but they talk about how

87
00:03:36,430 --> 00:03:38,890
when you're developing
a pesticide,

88
00:03:38,890 --> 00:03:41,740
the bugs will eventually

89
00:03:41,740 --> 00:03:43,390
thrive that are resistant to

90
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:45,010
whatever pesticide
you've created,

91
00:03:45,010 --> 00:03:47,515
and so it's a constant
cat and mouse game

92
00:03:47,515 --> 00:03:50,680
to keep creating
pesticides that are

93
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:53,560
able to resist pests that

94
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:56,720
have developed immunity
to previous pesticides,

95
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:58,990
and it works similarly with

96
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:01,480
testing in the sense
that if you focus only

97
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,265
on one particular area

98
00:04:03,265 --> 00:04:06,265
to test with your
automated testing,

99
00:04:06,265 --> 00:04:08,945
and you ignore new areas,

100
00:04:08,945 --> 00:04:10,700
then you'll never
have bugs in the area

101
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:12,755
that you've been
focusing on testing,

102
00:04:12,755 --> 00:04:14,570
but you'll have all kinds of

103
00:04:14,570 --> 00:04:16,580
bugs in the new areas
that aren't covered.

104
00:04:16,580 --> 00:04:19,490
You need to be continually
developing your tests to match

105
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:21,575
the implementation that you're

106
00:04:21,575 --> 00:04:22,790
adding are the features that

107
00:04:22,790 --> 00:04:24,610
you're adding to your software.

108
00:04:24,610 --> 00:04:26,930
You need to think about
the context when you're

109
00:04:26,930 --> 00:04:29,480
deciding the scope of the
test and how to test.

110
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:32,690
A safety critical software
for an airliner is not

111
00:04:32,690 --> 00:04:33,830
going to be tested the same as

112
00:04:33,830 --> 00:04:35,770
a commercial mobile application.

113
00:04:35,770 --> 00:04:38,660
If you can live with defects in

114
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:41,180
some areas that might influence

115
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:44,180
the amount of test-time that
you want to devote to it,

116
00:04:44,180 --> 00:04:45,590
the amount of retest you do,

117
00:04:45,590 --> 00:04:47,695
and new releases et cetera.

118
00:04:47,695 --> 00:04:50,460
Absence of errors is a fallacy,

119
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:53,330
in other words if you're
finding and fixing bugs,

120
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:54,680
but it doesn't really
tell you anything

121
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:55,730
about whether or not

122
00:04:55,730 --> 00:04:58,910
the software achieves it
is the actual objectives.

123
00:04:58,910 --> 00:05:00,050
In order to know that you

124
00:05:00,050 --> 00:05:01,130
need to know that the tests were

125
00:05:01,130 --> 00:05:04,500
actually designed to catch
those problems to begin with.

126
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:08,410
A couple of
definitions that we'll

127
00:05:08,410 --> 00:05:10,180
use when we're talking
about software testing.

128
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:11,995
The first is a test case.

129
00:05:11,995 --> 00:05:14,080
A test case is a set
of preconditions.

130
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:16,150
How do you set up the test run?

131
00:05:16,150 --> 00:05:19,690
A set of inputs and actions
that you would take in order

132
00:05:19,690 --> 00:05:23,590
to create scenarios and
unexpected results,

133
00:05:23,590 --> 00:05:25,120
what you should
see as a result of

134
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:27,760
those inputs and actions and
then the post-conditions,

135
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:31,330
how should the system be set
up after the test completes?

136
00:05:31,330 --> 00:05:33,730
A test execution is

137
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:35,980
an activity that would
run that test case

138
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:37,930
on a specific component

139
00:05:37,930 --> 00:05:40,330
or a system and
produce those results.

140
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:42,025
That could be linked to

141
00:05:42,025 --> 00:05:44,050
a specific version
of the software.

142
00:05:44,050 --> 00:05:45,340
In our case, since we're often

143
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:46,540
working with hardware devices,

144
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:47,710
it could also be linked to

145
00:05:47,710 --> 00:05:49,615
a specific version
of the hardware,

146
00:05:49,615 --> 00:05:51,490
and it's probably related to

147
00:05:51,490 --> 00:05:53,965
specific versions of
external dependencies.

148
00:05:53,965 --> 00:05:55,510
Some of the examples
that we've given

149
00:05:55,510 --> 00:05:57,715
this class where you're
interacting with a Cloud server.

150
00:05:57,715 --> 00:06:00,760
Maybe it's the version
of software running on

151
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:05,095
that Cloud server or interacting
with any other device.

152
00:06:05,095 --> 00:06:08,620
For instance, the version
of the software running on

153
00:06:08,620 --> 00:06:09,850
those devices might also be

154
00:06:09,850 --> 00:06:13,000
important to link to your
test case and execution.

155
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,150
Then a test suite is a
set of scripts or tests

156
00:06:16,150 --> 00:06:19,570
procedures to be executed
for a specific test run.

157
00:06:19,570 --> 00:06:22,390
A lot of times you'll
see tests functionally

158
00:06:22,390 --> 00:06:25,470
grouped into the specific
area they're targeting.

159
00:06:25,470 --> 00:06:27,735
If you're making a
change to the web UI,

160
00:06:27,735 --> 00:06:30,420
there might be a test suite
that you can run that's

161
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:31,890
a set of tests that test

162
00:06:31,890 --> 00:06:33,560
everything related
to the web UI.

163
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:35,875
If you're doing a major
overhaul of web UI,

164
00:06:35,875 --> 00:06:37,930
it's probably important that
you retest every one of

165
00:06:37,930 --> 00:06:41,210
those specific test cases
in that test suite.

166
00:06:42,210 --> 00:06:46,105
Some of the different levels
that you can test to.

167
00:06:46,105 --> 00:06:50,050
The first one is
component testing.

168
00:06:50,050 --> 00:06:51,460
This is where you can test

169
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:53,905
individual software
components in isolation.

170
00:06:53,905 --> 00:06:55,330
A lot of times the
developer that

171
00:06:55,330 --> 00:06:56,800
wrote the code is
going to do it.

172
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,150
It's like the testing that you

173
00:06:58,150 --> 00:06:59,590
perform on your bench before you

174
00:06:59,590 --> 00:07:02,770
decide that your code is
ready to share with others.

175
00:07:02,770 --> 00:07:04,360
Then the integration testing

176
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:06,685
would be the interaction between

177
00:07:06,685 --> 00:07:11,305
individual components
and or larger systems.

178
00:07:11,305 --> 00:07:13,780
How does your code
interact once you put it

179
00:07:13,780 --> 00:07:18,640
in use with the larger
overall system?

180
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:22,540
Then system testing is the
behavior and capabilities of

181
00:07:22,540 --> 00:07:24,670
an entire system of products so

182
00:07:24,670 --> 00:07:27,025
end-to-end tasks and
functional behaviors.

183
00:07:27,025 --> 00:07:29,875
In other words, if I'm interacting
with the Cloud server,

184
00:07:29,875 --> 00:07:32,140
how does it look from
my user perspective if

185
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:33,430
I'm actually interacting with

186
00:07:33,430 --> 00:07:34,990
the device through
the Cloud server?

187
00:07:34,990 --> 00:07:36,520
What does the user see?

188
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:38,080
What is the end result of

189
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:40,480
the interaction with the device?

190
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:44,170
Then acceptance testing is
the system testing that's

191
00:07:44,170 --> 00:07:47,665
used to gauge by the customer.

192
00:07:47,665 --> 00:07:51,580
Rather than a tester hired

193
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:53,335
by you or maybe someone in

194
00:07:53,335 --> 00:07:55,525
your development team
running a test case.

195
00:07:55,525 --> 00:07:57,400
This would be a customer

196
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:59,350
that's actually using
your product in

197
00:07:59,350 --> 00:08:01,270
a real-world
environment and giving

198
00:08:01,270 --> 00:08:03,640
you feedback on the
implementation.

199
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:05,500
You've been trying
to assimilate how

200
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:07,600
a customer might use
it with a test case,

201
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:09,910
but the customer is going to
have their own way of using

202
00:08:09,910 --> 00:08:11,110
it that might be
slightly different

203
00:08:11,110 --> 00:08:12,535
than what you were expecting.

204
00:08:12,535 --> 00:08:14,080
This would be an
example of where

205
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:16,165
the customer could
provide feedback on

206
00:08:16,165 --> 00:08:19,090
whether it's working
in their specific use

207
00:08:19,090 --> 00:08:20,740
example and if there's ways

208
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:22,555
to improve the user experience.

209
00:08:22,555 --> 00:08:23,920
You often hear this referred

210
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:26,050
to as Alpha and Beta testing.

211
00:08:26,050 --> 00:08:28,600
Getting your device
in customers hands

212
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:29,770
and getting some feedback from

213
00:08:29,770 --> 00:08:31,940
them about the functionality.

214
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:35,350
Some of the strategies that I'd

215
00:08:35,350 --> 00:08:37,480
suggest in terms
of manual testing,

216
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:38,890
you want to use the test case

217
00:08:38,890 --> 00:08:42,025
to record what you're
going to plan to do.

218
00:08:42,025 --> 00:08:43,300
A lot of times it's helpful to

219
00:08:43,300 --> 00:08:44,680
write these while
you're actually going

220
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:46,015
through the test yourself

221
00:08:46,015 --> 00:08:48,550
and think about a
step-by-step detail.

222
00:08:48,550 --> 00:08:50,125
Rather than just writing,

223
00:08:50,125 --> 00:08:51,910
doing some action,

224
00:08:51,910 --> 00:08:53,560
put down the steps that are

225
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:55,105
associated with
doing that action.

226
00:08:55,105 --> 00:08:57,055
Open some specific
page on the UI,

227
00:08:57,055 --> 00:08:59,080
click on some
specific button and

228
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:01,270
write down that level of detail.

229
00:09:01,270 --> 00:09:04,420
What, you don't want to do
is require someone to have

230
00:09:04,420 --> 00:09:05,740
your level of experience with

231
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:07,915
the software in order
to run the test case,

232
00:09:07,915 --> 00:09:10,030
you want to have
someone other than you

233
00:09:10,030 --> 00:09:12,250
as a developer be able
to run this test case.

234
00:09:12,250 --> 00:09:14,080
If you don't do
this, then you will

235
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:16,405
likely be the only one who
can run the test case.

236
00:09:16,405 --> 00:09:18,040
It's probably not what you want

237
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:20,860
to always be on the hook
for running this test case.

238
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:22,570
You need to think
about how you can

239
00:09:22,570 --> 00:09:25,000
design the software and
also how you can design

240
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,445
a test case such that
anyone's going to be able to

241
00:09:27,445 --> 00:09:30,850
help verify the implementation.

242
00:09:30,850 --> 00:09:33,310
Then use the test case execution

243
00:09:33,310 --> 00:09:35,305
to record what you did and when.

244
00:09:35,305 --> 00:09:37,195
Which software where you using,

245
00:09:37,195 --> 00:09:39,880
which versions of any
of the dependencies or

246
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:41,770
the external devices or

247
00:09:41,770 --> 00:09:43,570
applications that
you use to test,

248
00:09:43,570 --> 00:09:46,270
record that and keep
a history of when

249
00:09:46,270 --> 00:09:48,100
this particular action last

250
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:52,340
succeeded or if it
failed, how did it fail?

251
00:09:53,850 --> 00:09:56,170
Shifting gears and talking about

252
00:09:56,170 --> 00:09:58,840
automated testing
instead of manual test.

253
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:01,030
The first step, usually
with automated testing

254
00:10:01,030 --> 00:10:03,310
is to make the build
automated because

255
00:10:03,310 --> 00:10:06,880
it's maybe less useful to
have an automated test if you

256
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:10,510
need to do manual deployment
and update of your code.

257
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:12,260
A lot of times,
the way you start

258
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:13,640
automated testing is by getting

259
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:15,420
an automated build
framework going.

260
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:17,955
We'll talk about some
strategies for doing that.

261
00:10:17,955 --> 00:10:21,235
Then you'll also merge

